findings.detail:
The latest TimescaleDB release is 2.30.2 (2026-09-29). Version 2.30.0 added the DeferredChunkAppend scan node, which speeds up LIMIT and last-point queries. The latest Toolkit release is 1.26.0 (2026-09-01), per https://raw.githubusercontent.com/timescale/timescaledb-toolkit/main/CHANGELOG.md. Tiger Cloud has run Postgres 18 by default for all new services since 2026-03-16 (https://www.tigerdata.com/blog/whats-new-tiger-cloud-faster-performance-easier-workflows-simpler-adoption). The extensions table at https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/use-timescale/latest/extensions lists timescaledb, timescaledb_toolkit and pg_stat_statements as 'Enabled by default' in every Tiger Cloud service. ca-central-1 (Canada Central) is a supported AWS region (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/get-started/supported-regions). Free services are hosted only in us-east-1 and have reduced features (no metrics, tiering, pooling and so on), per https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/deploy/tiger-cloud/tiger-cloud-aws/service-management. A ca-central-1 service is therefore the trial service: '$1000-credit, 30-day trial of our Performance or Scale plan' (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/about/latest/pricing-and-account-management).
Run these checks:
SELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension WHERE extname IN ('timescaledb','timescaledb_toolkit');
SHOW server_version;
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS timescaledb_toolkit;

Function signature (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/continuous-aggregates/add_continuous_aggregate_policy):
SELECT add_continuous_aggregate_policy(continuous_aggregate, start_offset, end_offset, schedule_interval, if_not_exists, initial_start, timezone, include_tiered_data, buckets_per_batch, max_batches_per_execution, refresh_newest_first)
- It is a FUNCTION, so call it with SELECT. It returns job_id.
- start_offset must be greater than end_offset.
- end_offset => NULL is not recommended; use real-time aggregation instead.
The source code enforces 'policy refresh window too small ... The start and end offsets must cover at least two buckets' (https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb/blob/main/tsl/src/bgw_policy/continuous_aggregate_api.c).
refresh_continuous_aggregate is a PROCEDURE: CALL refresh_continuous_aggregate(cagg, window_start, window_end, force => ..., options => ...). The options argument (buckets_per_batch, refresh_newest_first) is new in 2.28.0. Only buckets wholly inside the window are refreshed (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/continuous-aggregates/refresh_continuous_aggregate). psycopg needs autocommit for 'CALL on stored procedures using transaction control' (https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/basic/transactions.html).
SQL:
SELECT add_continuous_aggregate_policy('hr_1m', start_offset => INTERVAL '2 hours', end_offset => INTERVAL '1 minute', schedule_interval => INTERVAL '1 minute');
SELECT add_continuous_aggregate_policy('hr_1h', start_offset => INTERVAL '1 day', end_offset => INTERVAL '1 hour', schedule_interval => INTERVAL '10 minutes');
SELECT add_continuous_aggregate_policy('hr_1d', start_offset => INTERVAL '3 days', end_offset => INTERVAL '1 day', schedule_interval => INTERVAL '1 hour');
(Repeat for the accel_* views.)
One-off backfill, bottom layer first:
CALL refresh_continuous_aggregate('hr_1m', now() - INTERVAL '8 days', now());
CALL refresh_continuous_aggregate('hr_1h', now() - INTERVAL '8 days', now());
CALL refresh_continuous_aggregate('hr_1d', now() - INTERVAL '8 days', now());

add_columnstore_policy is a PROCEDURE: CALL add_columnstore_policy(hypertable, after | created_before, if_not_exists, schedule_interval, initial_start, timezone). It has been available since 2.18.0 and replaces add_compression_policy, which was deprecated in 2.18.0. created_before is not supported on caggs. Settings apply only to chunks not yet converted (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/hypercore/add_columnstore_policy). The Postgres source declares 'CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE add_columnstore_policy' and 'remove_columnstore_policy', while add_retention_policy and add_continuous_aggregate_policy are FUNCTIONs (https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb/blob/main/sql/policy_api.sql). The 'data lifecycle' docs page shows SELECT add_columnstore_policy(...); that is wrong, use CALL. compress_chunk(regclass, if_not_compressed, recompress) is still a FUNCTION, deprecated, and convert_to_columnstore is its CALL-only alias (https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb/blob/main/sql/maintenance_utils.sql).
SQL:
-- change the auto-created policy (only if needed)
CALL remove_columnstore_policy('accel_samples', if_exists => true);
CALL add_columnstore_policy('accel_samples', after => INTERVAL '1 day');
-- convert closed chunks now, for the demo
SELECT compress_chunk(c, if_not_compressed => true)
FROM show_chunks('accel_samples', older_than => INTERVAL '1 day') AS c;
-- or run the policy job immediately (run_job: https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/jobs-automation/run_job)
SELECT job_id FROM timescaledb_information.jobs WHERE proc_name = 'policy_compression' AND hypertable_name = 'accel_samples';
CALL run_job(<job_id>);
-- show the compression ratio (hypertable_columnstore_stats: https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/hypercore/hypertable_columnstore_stats)
SELECT total_chunks, number_compressed_chunks,
  pg_size_pretty(before_compression_total_bytes) AS before,
  pg_size_pretty(after_compression_total_bytes) AS after,
  round(before_compression_total_bytes::numeric / NULLIF(after_compression_total_bytes,0),1) AS ratio
FROM hypertable_columnstore_stats('accel_samples');

Retention: SELECT add_retention_policy(relation => <hypertable_or_cagg>, drop_after => INTERVAL ..., ...). It drops whole chunks, compressed or not, and also works on caggs (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/reference/timescaledb/data-retention/add_retention_policy). The docs' complete example combines columnstore after 7 days, a daily cagg, tiering and retention on one table (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/learn/data-management/data-lifecycle). The add_tiering_policy step in that example is Scale-only and should be skipped.
Rules:
(1) A source table's retention must be longer than the refresh window (start_offset) of any cagg reading it. Otherwise the refresh 'sees that the raw data was deleted' and 'also deletes the aggregate data' (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/learn/data-lifecycle/data-retention/data-retention-with-continuous-aggregates).
(2) A manual refresh over a dropped range also erases those aggregates (https://tigerdata.com/docs/build/continuous-aggregates/refresh-policies).
(3) Columnstore on a cagg needs, since 2.20.0, ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW <cagg> SET (timescaledb.enable_columnstore = true), and its 'after' must be greater than that cagg's refresh start_offset. Since 2.27.0 you can instead set compress_after_refresh on the refresh job (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/build/continuous-aggregates/compression-on-continuous-aggregates).
(4) Inserts into compressed chunks work without manual decompression (https://www.tigerdata.com/docs/build/data-management/write-data/insert).
SQL for StillHere:
SELECT add_retention_policy('accel_samples', drop_after => INTERVAL '3 days');
SELECT add_retention_policy('hr_samples', drop_after => INTERVAL '3 days');
SELECT add_retention_policy('hr_1m', drop_after => INTERVAL '14 days');
SELECT add_retention_policy('accel_1m', drop_after => INTERVAL '14 days');
SELECT add_retention_policy('hr_1h', drop_after => INTERVAL '90 days');
-- hr_1d: keep
-- optional:
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW hr_1m SET (timescaledb.enable_columnstore = true, timescaledb.segmentby = 'device_id');
CALL add_columnstore_policy('hr_1m', after => INTERVAL '1 day');
These settings satisfy the rules: the 2 h refresh window of hr_1m is under 3 days of raw retention; the 1 day window of hr_1h is under 14 days of hr_1m retention; the 3 day window of hr_1d is under 90 days of hr_1h retention; and the 7-day z-score baseline (from hr_1m) is under 14 days.

findings.recommendation:
Endpoint POST /senior/cards/{card_id}/narrative. It sends the card JSON (rules, values, n, dates, limits) with this system prompt: 'Rewrite as a 2-sentence SBAR handoff for a nurse. Use only numbers present in the JSON. Do not name any disease or condition. Do not predict.'

Validate the reply before saving:
(1) every number in the output appears in the JSON;
(2) no blocklisted term appears;
(3) the reply is 60 words or fewer.

On failure, fall back to a template string. Store the model name and prompt hash in alert_card. Never use the LLM to change the tier.

Table:
CREATE TABLE stand_event (ts timestamptz NOT NULL, resident_id text NOT NULL, source text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'synthetic' CHECK (source IN ('synthetic','device')), hr_base real, hr_delta_30 real, hr_settle_s real, sway_settle_s real, walked_off boolean, detector_conf real, baro_dh_m real) WITH (tsdb.hypertable, tsdb.segmentby = 'resident_id');

If your service rejects the WITH form, create a plain table and run SELECT create_hypertable('stand_event', by_range('ts')); (older API; I did not re-verify it).

Continuous aggregate:
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW stand_daily WITH (timescaledb.continuous) AS SELECT resident_id, time_bucket('1 day', ts) AS day, count(*) AS n, stats_agg(hr_settle_s) AS hr_settle_ss, percentile_agg(hr_settle_s) AS hr_settle_pct, percentile_agg(sway_settle_s) AS sway_settle_pct, stats_agg(hr_delta_30) AS hr_d30_ss FROM stand_event GROUP BY resident_id, day WITH NO DATA;
SELECT add_continuous_aggregate_policy('stand_daily', start_offset => INTERVAL '60 days', end_offset => INTERVAL '1 hour', schedule_interval => INTERVAL '15 minutes');
CALL refresh_continuous_aggregate('stand_daily', NULL, NULL);

R1 query:
SELECT resident_id, approx_percentile(0.5, rollup(hr_settle_pct) FILTER (WHERE day >= %(as_of)s - INTERVAL '7 days')) AS recent_med, approx_percentile(0.5, rollup(hr_settle_pct) FILTER (WHERE day < %(as_of)s - INTERVAL '7 days')) AS base_med, sum(n) FILTER (WHERE day >= %(as_of)s - INTERVAL '7 days') AS recent_n, sum(n) FILTER (WHERE day < %(as_of)s - INTERVAL '7 days') AS base_n FROM stand_daily WHERE day >= %(as_of)s - INTERVAL '35 days' AND day < %(as_of)s GROUP BY resident_id;

Rolling z-score for the Tiger demo:
SELECT resident_id, day, average(rolling(hr_d30_ss) OVER b) AS base_mean, stddev(rolling(hr_d30_ss) OVER b) AS base_sd, num_vals(rolling(hr_d30_ss) OVER b) AS base_n FROM stand_daily WINDOW b AS (PARTITION BY resident_id ORDER BY day RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '35 days' PRECEDING AND INTERVAL '8 days' PRECEDING);

Test the offset-frame window on your instance before relying on it. Keep resident, rule_config, alert_card (rules jsonb, tier, points, narrative, ack_by, ack_at, ack_outcome) and sim.truth as plain Postgres tables. Mirror gait_daily and night_summary as hypertables in the same pattern.
